these secret files offer a rare glimpse  at iran's alleged ambitions for new  cyber attacks  possible targets include cargo ships and  petrol stations  britain the united states and france  among countries of apparent interest  the uk's defense secretary says if  authentic  the documents show how vulnerable the  real world is to attacks from cyberspace  unless we do something about it our  critical infrastructure  our way of life can be threatened quite  easily  and you know there are states out there  who are very active  in mapping hacking looking for  vulnerabilities  the five separate reports are marked  very confidential  and written by intelligence team 13  allegedly a secret iranian cyber group  they're not all dated but this one is  from the iranian calendars equivalent  of the 19th of november last year  most of the files start with a quote  apparently from iran's supreme leader  saying the islamic republic of iran must  become  among the world's most powerful in the  area of cyber  the papers then reveal fairly basic  research  into various civilian sectors including  here  the ballast water system on cargo ships  this diagram  shows how it helps a vessel to balance  or cap size if manipulated  it says any kind of disruptive influence  can cause significant  and irreparable damage to the vessel  another document  looks at a system that monitors fuel at  a petrol station  it notes how a cyber hack on the  automatic tank gauge  could cut off fuel supply or even cause  an explosion  iran was linked in 2017 to a cyber  attack on the uk parliament  thousands of email accounts were  targeted including those of mps  now these five documents appear to show  evidence of iranian cyber research into  other areas of civilian life  the source who shared them with me said  he believes the cell that compiled this  data  is creating a target bank to be used  whenever they see fit  the security source said intelligence  team 13  is part of a branch known as shahid cave  that's within the islamic revolutionary  guard course cyber command  iran's embassy in the uk did not respond  to a request for comment on the files  but tehran has in the past denied  allegations of cyber attacks  the general in charge of the uk's cyber  weapons  isn't surprised iran wants to boost its  cyber power  among the most advanced  cyber actors so we take their their  capabilities  seriously we don't um we don't over  state it but we  you know they're a serious actor and  they have behaved really irresponsibly  in the past  as these files indicate they may also be  seeking to pose a threat  in the future debra haynes sky news
 

